;; Calls of C functions: decision D50 as it is ruled, which the tool has
;; (see ../../tools/flsemantics.ml, "Calls of C functions"). A run is
;; undefined at a call of a C function of which the model says nothing, and
;; it says nothing of any yet: the tool makes of such a call a direct call
;; of code that the term declares and does not bind (the reading called (U)
;; in the request). The units and the pairs here are in none of the tables
;; and counts of ../ocaml/README.md. `dune promote` updates an X.expected.
;; The rules are written out below.
;;
;; UNITS FROM OCAML, each of the shape of a file of the standard library
;; that this reading lets the tool check. X.fl is the term that the
;; compiler gives Simplify of X.ml, as ../ocaml/from_ocaml.sh makes it, and
;; a rule checks that it still makes it; X.expected is what the tool prints
;; of X.fl.
;;
;; exported_externals (Condition, Dynamic), call_in_function (Int, Bool),
;; call_under_handler (Mutex), call_with_unboxed_result (Int32: a result
;; that is not a value): the run of each unit returns, and no call of a C
;; function is reached. The line of each is "validated with its imports".
;; In a term that the compiler makes, the callee of a call of a C function
;; is a symbol of the unit of external symbols, which is an import: so each
;; of these units has imports, and the words after "validated" are what the
;; tool's test of the imports gives for any unit that has some. These four
;; pass it. What the verdict says of the functions that call C functions is
;; that the two terms agree as the validation compares them; that rests on
;; the rewrites of the validation as they are written, and on no theorem
;; (the comment in the tool). exported_externals has an interface that says
;; val of its C functions, as those two files of the library have: the
;; compiler then makes a function for each, whose body is the call.
;;
;; call_at_toplevel: A CONTROL. The unit calls a C function as it is
;; initialised. Both runs are undefined at the call; a line says so of each
;; and names the C function. There is no verdict: the line is "nothing to
;; validate, as the term before is undefined", the validator's acceptance of
;; the pair is on the next line, and under that is "the term before is
;; undefined in the model, so the validation says nothing of this unit":
;; nothing of what comes after the call, and nothing of what comes before it.
;;
;; comparison_made_a_primitive: A CONTROL of what is left out. Simplify
;; makes a primitive of a call of caml_compare whose arguments it knows to
;; be integers; nothing relates the call to the primitive, and the unit is
;; NOT validated.
;;
;; PAIRS OF TERMS, given to the tool with the option -after (the term
;; after is read from a text and Simplify is not run). Every term after is
;; written by hand: it is the term before with one thing changed, and the
;; comment at its head says which. The terms name no code id and no slot,
;; which are what the reader of a term after numbers apart from the term
;; before (compare ../guard/, after_same_text, where a unit with code is not
;; validated against itself). A symbol is the same by its name. The code ids
;; of the C functions are made by the conversion from one table for both
;; terms, whose key is the name of the function and the four marks of the
;; call. So the validator ACCEPTS the pair X_same, which gives one text
;; twice. Four such pairs are validated (guarded_same,
;; guarded_generative_same, handled_same, two_calls_same); reached_same is
;; accepted and has no verdict, as its run before is found undefined (below).
;; Where a pair below with the same term before is not accepted, the one
;; change is why.
;;
;; In guarded.fl, handled.fl and two_calls.fl the call is in the arm of a
;; test that the run does not take, and the test is of an opaque value: the
;; run returns, and the call is compared and not run.
;;
;; guarded_other_function, guarded_other_argument: another C function; another
;; argument. NOT validated.
;;
;; guarded_noalloc, guarded_builtin, guarded_no_effects, guarded_no_coeffects:
;; the term after says of the C function one of the four things that the
;; compiler says of it at a call, and the term before does not. NOT
;; validated: the four are part of what the conversion keeps of the call.
;;
;; guarded_generative_same, guarded_generative_mutable: the one mark that
;; has a word after it. guarded_generative.fl says that the C function has
;; generative effects only, and its term after that what it makes is
;; mutable. NOT validated, and the two C functions are printed with their
;; words; the text given twice is.
;;
;; guarded_after_the_return: the terms differ only in what follows the return
;; of the call. handled_other_handler: only in the exception handler of the
;; call. two_calls_other_later_function, two_calls_other_later_argument: only
;; in a second call of a C function, after the first has returned. NOT
;; validated, none of them. These four are the controls of a hazard: the
;; model has no run past a call of a C function, so it says nothing of what
;; follows one, and a rewrite of the validation that dropped or changed what
;; follows such a call would be sound in the model. As the rewrites are
;; written, none does. These pairs show a change of the tool that makes one
;; of these four comparisons accepted. They do not show every such rewrite:
;; one that touches another shape of term, a function body, another arity or
;; another handler leaves them as they are. The test of the hazard inside a
;; function body is the compiler's own pair on a source, with a Simplify
;; built with a fault that changes what follows the return of a C call; that
;; is outside the tree.
;;
;; reached_same, reached_after_the_return: the run reaches the call
;; (reached.fl has it at toplevel) and is undefined there, and both have the
;; line "nothing to validate, as the term before is undefined". One text
;; given twice has under it the line of the validator's acceptance, which is
;; no verdict, and under that the line that the validation says nothing of
;; this unit. The pair that differs after the return has neither: the
;; validator does not accept it.
;;
;; A FIRST UNIT AND ITS SECOND UNITS, compiled as ../ocaml/pairs/ compiles
;; a pair (its from_ocaml.sh): the second unit against the .cmi and the .cmx
;; of the first, and the tool run on the second with that .cmx at hand. They
;; are the controls of what the reading changes IN FRONT of a unit: the
;; body of a function of another unit that calls a C function, where the
;; .cmx keeps it, is now bound there, where its code was declared deleted.
;; The tool is not run on a first unit.
;;
;; body_with_a_call, a function that calls a C function in one arm.
;; body_with_a_call_reached calls it in that arm: the run enters the body
;; and is undefined at the call of the C function, which is named, in the
;; term before and in the term after. body_with_a_call_not_reached calls it
;; in the other arm: the run enters the body and returns, and the unit is
;; validated with its imports. With the code declared deleted, its run is
;; undefined at the call of the body, and there is nothing to validate.
;;
;; body_with_a_store, a function that calls a C function and stores what it
;; returns in a cell. Its two second units name that function, do not call
;; it, and define a function of their own. A lifting to symbols lifts nothing
;; in a term that has a call outside the bodies of its code and a primitive
;; of the heap in the code bound in front of it (decision D65: where a term
;; has no such call, the two tests of the liftings look outside those bodies
;; only). body_with_a_store_and_a_call calls its own function at top level,
;; through the primitive %opaque, so that the call stays in both terms and is
;; not a call of known code (the tool counts it still once the calls of known
;; code are inlined). It is NOT validated, and the tool prints the line of
;; the guard. With the code of the body declared deleted the unit is validated
;; with its imports (the tool as it was before this reading, run on this text
;; on 19 November 2026; no rule here runs that tool). THIS IS A UNIT THAT
;; CALLS NO C FUNCTION AND LOSES ITS VERDICT BY THE READING.
;; body_with_a_store_not_called calls nothing. It was not validated before
;; decision D65, for the same primitive of the heap; it is validated with its
;; imports since (by its expected file, before the finer tests and with
;; them), and its output carries the mark of that decision (the tool's line
;; that begins "(decision D65:", under the verdict line).
;;
;; tupled_body_with_a_call, a function of a pair that calls a C function:
;; tupled code, which the conversion does not bind from another unit. Its
;; body is converted, and then the code is declared deleted.
;; tupled_body_with_a_call_named names the function and calls nothing. A
;; CONTROL of what is declared in front of a unit: nothing in front of this
;; one calls the C function, so it is not declared, and the output has no
;; line of a C function. The lines of its prefix are those of a unit that
;; has nothing to do with C functions.
;;
;; A CONTROL OF A REFUSAL. callee_bound_in_the_text, a text written by hand
;; and given twice with -after: the callee of its call of a C function is a
;; symbol that the text itself binds as a block, and not a symbol of the
;; unit of external symbols. The conversion refuses such a call, and the
;; text is NOT CHECKED. No compiler makes such a call; every other callee of
;; this directory is a symbol of that unit. Before the refusal this text had
;; the C function of that name declared in front and the symbol bound in the
;; term, no import, and the line "validated: the same up to bound names"
;; (run on 18 November 2026).
;;
;; THE ROUND TRIP of the conversion (../roundtrip/README.md), on the
;; sixteen texts here that are units by themselves: the six units from
;; OCaml, the three first units, the five terms before of the pairs, the
;; text of the refusal, and every_mark, which is a text for this rule alone.
;; The rules of ../roundtrip/ do not take this directory. The printer of the
;; model writes a call of a C function back as one, with what the compiler
;; says of the function, and the round trip compares the C function that is
;; read back with the one that was printed (the comment in the tool, "What
;; the round trip tests of it"). round_trip.expected has two lines for each
;; text, of the unit of the file and of the unit that Simplify makes of it:
;; "round trip the same" in each, but for callee_bound_in_the_text, which
;; the conversion refuses. every_mark has a call for each of the things
;; that the compiler says of a C function at a call, and two calls of one
;; function that say different things: two C functions for the conversion,
;; with one callee. The rule takes neither the terms after of the pairs, on
;; which Simplify cannot be run by themselves (each binds the symbol of its
;; term before), nor the second units, which would be read here without the
;; .cmx of their first unit. With a printer changed to write the opposite
;; of what the term says of whether the function is called through
;; caml_c_call, the lines of call_in_function are "names not read back: the
;; call of a C function at the callee x4 is not, read back, a call of the C
;; function that was printed" (tried on 18 November 2026; no rule runs that
;; printer).
;;
;; BOUNDS. Nothing in the tree checks, of the units from OCaml here, that
;; their text is the compiler's term (../text_is_term/), or runs the
;; compiler's own pair on them. "Round trip the same" does not say that a C
;; function has its name in the table of the conversion: the printer does
;; not write the name.

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  round_trip.output
  (progn
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:call_at_toplevel.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:call_in_function.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:call_under_handler.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:call_with_unboxed_result.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:comparison_made_a_primitive.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:exported_externals.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:body_with_a_call.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:body_with_a_store.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:tupled_body_with_a_call.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:guarded.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:guarded_generative.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:handled.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:reached.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:two_calls.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:callee_bound_in_the_text.fl})
   (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -round-trip %{dep:every_mark.fl}))))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff round_trip.expected round_trip.output)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  guarded_same.output
  (run
   ../../tools/flsemantics.exe
   -after
   %{dep:guarded.fl}
   %{dep:guarded.fl})))

